SYSTEM OVERVIEW
===============
The system is a Role-based Access Control (RBAC) system designed for a multi-user environment with support for role hierarchies, temporary role delegation, and concurrent request processing. Each user is assigned a security clearance level (High, Medium, Low) and may hold assigned or delegated roles constrained by clearance requirements. Users submit action requests affecting system objects classified by security levels, with access and processing governed by clearance and classification rules. The system ensures authorization correctness, delegation and permission consistency, liveness of request processing, and safety under concurrency. Additionally, an Emergency Module is introduced to handle emergency conditions through a state transition to Emergency mode, enabling prioritized request processing and temporary changes in role and privilege assignments, reverting back to Normal mode after a limited time or manual termination. Multiple successive Emergency mode activations are supported.

EXISTING-SYSTEM ASSUMPTIONS AND CONSTRAINTS
============================================
- The system operates concurrently, processing requests in FIFO order during Normal mode, modeled abstractly via timestamps and ordering facts without explicit request queue structures.
- Users have one of three clearance levels: High, Medium, or Low.
- Administrators are users with High clearance, but not all High-clearance users are administrators.
- Users hold roles (assigned or delegated), with clearance constraints enforcing minimum clearance for role holding.
- Requests affect one or more system objects, each with a classification level; request classification equals the highest classification of affected objects.
- Users may submit requests only if the request classification is less than or equal to their clearance.
- Higher-clearance users can approve or reject requests from lower-clearance users.
- Role delegation is temporary, allowed only from higher-clearance users to lower-clearance users meeting the role’s minimum clearance.
- Delegated roles expire after a fixed time or can be revoked by administrators at any time.
- The system guarantees eventual processing of every submitted request (liveness).
- Authorization correctness, delegation and permission consistency, and safety under concurrency are preserved.

PROSPECTIVE FUNCTIONAL REQUIREMENTS
====================================
R1: Emergency Triggering  
While operating in Normal mode, exactly two administrators are required to jointly trigger an emergency, causing the system to enter Emergency mode immediately when both administrators trigger within the exact same Time atom. Multiple successive Emergency mode activations are supported.

R2: Emergency Duration  
Emergency mode is active only for a fixed (predetermined) duration, with an option to manually end the Emergency mode early by authorized administrators; after which the system must automatically return to Normal mode if not ended manually before.

R3: Emergency Mode Behavior  
During Emergency mode:  
  R3.1: Request processing may bypass the usual FIFO order to prioritize processing requests by classification level in strict order: first all High-classification requests concurrently, then all Medium-classification requests concurrently, followed by all Low-classification requests concurrently.  
  R3.2: Users are allowed to temporarily hold mutually exclusive roles, relaxing the exclusivity constraints enforced during Normal mode by completely suspending these constraints.  
  R3.3: Administrators temporarily gain emergency privileges exclusive to Emergency mode, which cannot be held outside Emergency mode.  
  R3.4: Delegated roles can be assigned as usual, respecting existing clearance and delegation constraints. Delegated roles assigned during Emergency mode may be revoked at any time before Emergency mode ends.

R4: Post-Emergency Restoration  
After Emergency mode ends:  
  R4.1: All emergency privileges held by administrators must be revoked immediately.  
  R4.2: All roles delegated during Emergency mode must be revoked. Delegated roles assigned prior to Emergency mode remain active unless expired or revoked by an administrator.  
  R4.3: Administrators must update their credentials immediately after Emergency mode ends. This credential update is an atomic, blocking process that must be completed by both administrators before any further system interaction.

KEY CONCEPTS AND ENTITIES
==========================
- User: An entity representing a system user, assigned a clearance level and roles (assigned or delegated).  
- Administrator: A user subtype with High clearance and additional administrative privileges.  
- Role: A set of permissions assigned to users; roles have clearance requirements and exclusivity constraints defined as static pairs of mutually exclusive roles.  
- Delegated Role: A temporary role assigned from a higher-clearance user to a lower-clearance user, subject to expiration or revocation.  
- Security Clearance Level: An ordered level (High > Medium > Low) assigned to users determining access rights.  
- System Object: An entity affected by actions; each has a classification level (High, Medium, Low).  
- Request: A user-submitted action affecting one or more system objects, classified by the highest classification of those objects.  
- Emergency Mode: A system operational mode triggered by exactly two administrators simultaneously within the exact same Time atom, enabling special behaviors and privileges for a fixed duration or until manually ended early. Multiple successive activations are possible.  
- Emergency Privilege: A temporary privilege granted to administrators during Emergency mode, exclusive to that mode and revoked immediately upon its conclusion.  
- Role Exclusivity: Constraints preventing users from holding mutually exclusive roles simultaneously (defined as static pairs), which are completely suspended during Emergency mode.  
- FIFO Order: The default ordering of request processing during Normal mode, modeled abstractly via timestamps and ordering facts without explicit request queue structures.  
- Mode: The system operational state, either Normal or Emergency.

