SYSTEM OVERVIEW
===============
The system is a Role-based Access Control (RBAC) system designed for a multi-user environment with support for role hierarchies, temporary role delegation, and concurrent request processing. Each user is assigned a security clearance level (High, Medium, Low) and may hold assigned or delegated roles constrained by clearance requirements. Users submit action requests affecting system objects classified by security levels, with access and processing governed by clearance and classification rules. The system ensures authorization correctness, delegation and permission consistency, liveness of request processing, and safety under concurrency. Additionally, an Emergency Module is introduced to handle emergency conditions through a state transition to Emergency mode, enabling prioritized request processing and temporary changes in role and privilege assignments, reverting back to Normal mode after a limited time or manual termination. Multiple successive Emergency mode activations are supported.

EXISTING-SYSTEM ASSUMPTIONS AND CONSTRAINTS
============================================
- The system operates concurrently, processing requests in FIFO order during Normal mode, modeled abstractly via timestamps and ordering facts without explicit request queue structures.
- Users have one of three clearance levels: High, Medium, or Low.
- Administrators are users with High clearance, but not all High-clearance users are administrators.
- Users hold roles (assigned or delegated), with clearance constraints enforcing minimum clearance for role holding.
- Requests affect one or more system objects, each with a classification level; request classification equals the highest classification of affected objects.
- Users may submit requests only if the request classification is less than or equal to their clearance.
- Higher-clearance users can approve or reject requests from lower-clearance users.
- Role delegation is temporary, allowed only from higher-clearance users to lower-clearance users meeting the role’s minimum clearance.
- Delegated roles expire after a fixed time or can be revoked by administrators at any time.
- The system guarantees eventual processing of every submitted request (liveness).
- Authorization correctness, delegation and permission consistency, and safety under concurrency are preserved.
- FIFO ordering of requests during Normal mode is modeled abstractly by timestamps and ordering facts, without explicit request queue structures.

PROSPECTIVE FUNCTIONAL REQUIREMENTS
====================================
R1: Emergency Triggering  
While operating in Normal mode, exactly two administrators are required to jointly trigger an emergency, causing the system to enter Emergency mode immediately when both administrators trigger within the exact same Time atom. This simultaneous triggering event is atomic and cannot be interleaved with other triggers. Multiple successive Emergency mode activations are supported.

R2: Emergency Duration  
Emergency mode is active only for a fixed (predetermined) duration, with an option to manually end the Emergency mode early by authorized administrators; the system must return to Normal mode immediately upon duration expiry or manual termination.

R3: Emergency Mode Behavior  
During Emergency mode:  
  R3.1: Request processing may bypass the usual FIFO order to prioritize processing requests by classification level in strict order: first all High-classification requests concurrently, then all Medium-classification requests concurrently, followed by all Low-classification requests concurrently. Overlapping processing times within each classification batch are allowed; however, no requests of a lower classification may begin processing before completion of all requests of a higher classification batch.  
  R3.2: Users are allowed to temporarily hold mutually exclusive roles, relaxing the exclusivity constraints enforced during Normal mode by completely suspending these constraints.  
  R3.3: Administrators temporarily gain emergency privileges exclusive to Emergency mode, which cannot be granted or held outside Emergency mode. These privileges are granted once at Emergency mode start and revoked immediately upon its conclusion; however, revocation can also occur at any time during Emergency mode if necessary.  
  R3.4: Delegated roles can be assigned as usual, respecting existing clearance and delegation constraints. Delegated roles assigned during Emergency mode may be revoked at any time before Emergency mode ends.

R4: Post-Emergency Restoration  
After Emergency mode ends:  
  R4.1: All emergency privileges held by administrators must be revoked immediately and completely.  
  R4.2: All roles delegated during Emergency mode must be revoked immediately upon Emergency mode exit. Delegated roles assigned prior to Emergency mode remain active unless expired or revoked by an administrator.  
  R4.3: Administrators must update their credentials immediately after Emergency mode ends. This credential update is a mandatory, atomic, blocking process that must be completed by both administrators before any further system interaction is permitted. This update is performed once per Emergency mode exit and is modeled abstractly as a requirement check.

KEY CONCEPTS AND ENTITIES
==========================
- User: An entity representing a system user, assigned a clearance level and roles (assigned or delegated).  
- Administrator: A user subtype with High clearance and additional administrative privileges.  
- Role: A set of permissions assigned to users; roles have clearance requirements and exclusivity constraints defined as static pairs of mutually exclusive roles.  
- Delegated Role: A temporary role assigned from a higher-clearance user to a lower-clearance user, subject to expiration or revocation.  
- Security Clearance Level: An ordered level (High > Medium > Low) assigned to users determining access rights.  
- System Object: An entity affected by actions; each has a classification level (High, Medium, Low).  
- Request: A user-submitted action affecting one or more system objects, classified by the highest classification of those objects.  
- Emergency Mode: A system operational mode triggered by exactly two administrators simultaneously within the exact same Time atom, enabling special behaviors and privileges for a fixed duration or until manually ended early. Multiple successive activations are possible.  
- Emergency Privilege: A temporary privilege granted to administrators during Emergency mode, exclusive to that mode, granted once at Emergency mode start, revocable during Emergency mode, and revoked immediately upon its conclusion. Cannot be held outside Emergency mode.  
- Role Exclusivity: Constraints preventing users from holding mutually exclusive roles simultaneously (defined as static pairs), which are completely suspended during Emergency mode.  
- FIFO Order: The default ordering of request processing during Normal mode, modeled abstractly via timestamps and ordering facts without explicit request queue structures.  
- Mode: The system operational state, either Normal or Emergency.  
- Credential Update: An atomic, blocking, mandatory process performed by administrators immediately after Emergency mode ends, required before any further system interaction.

ASSUMPTIONS REQUIRING CLARIFICATION
====================================
1. The system transitions immediately to Emergency mode as soon as exactly two administrators trigger the emergency simultaneously within the exact same Time atom; this simultaneous triggering is atomic and cannot be interleaved with other triggers.  
2. Emergency mode has a fixed, predetermined duration, but also supports manual early termination by authorized administrators; the system returns to Normal mode immediately upon duration expiry or manual termination.  
3. Mutually exclusive roles are defined as static pairs of roles; during Emergency mode, exclusivity constraints are completely suspended, allowing users to hold mutually exclusive roles simultaneously.  
4. Updating credentials by administrators after Emergency mode is a mandatory, atomic, blocking action required immediately after Emergency ends and must be completed by both administrators once per Emergency mode exit before any further system interaction.  
5. Delegated roles can continue to be assigned during Emergency mode as usual. Delegated roles assigned during Emergency mode may be revoked at any time before Emergency mode ends. Upon exiting Emergency mode, delegated roles assigned during Emergency mode are revoked immediately; delegated roles assigned prior to Emergency mode remain unless expired or explicitly revoked by an administrator.  
6. Administrators cannot hold Emergency privileges outside Emergency mode. Emergency privileges are granted once at Emergency mode start, may be revoked during Emergency mode if necessary, and must be revoked immediately upon Emergency mode conclusion.  
7. FIFO ordering is modeled abstractly via timestamps and ordering facts without explicit request queue structures.  
8. During Emergency mode, requests are processed in classification-prioritized concurrent batches: all High first, then all Medium, then all Low; no lower classification requests start processing before completion of all higher classification requests; overlapping processing within each classification batch is allowed.  
9. If Emergency mode is manually ended exactly at the boundary of the fixed duration, the system immediately transitions to Normal mode, revoking Emergency privileges and performing all post-Emergency restoration steps without delay.

CONSTRAINTS
===========
- During Normal mode, request processing strictly follows FIFO order, modeled abstractly via timestamps and ordering facts.  
- Users can only submit requests with classification less than or equal to their clearance.  
- Role delegation must respect clearance constraints and is temporary with expiration or revocation possible at any time.  
- Exactly two administrators are required simultaneously (within the exact same Time atom) to trigger Emergency mode; no fewer or more administrators can trigger it alone; simultaneous triggering is atomic and cannot be interleaved.  
- Emergency mode duration is finite and fixed, with support for manual early termination; the system must return to Normal mode immediately afterward.  
- During Emergency mode, requests are processed in classification-prioritized concurrent batches: all High first, then all Medium, then all Low; requests within each classification batch may be processed concurrently with overlapping times, but strictly no processing of a lower classification batch starts before the higher batch completes.  
- Mutual exclusivity of roles is completely suspended during Emergency mode, allowing users to hold such roles temporarily.  
- Emergency privileges granted to administrators are exclusive to Emergency mode, granted once at Emergency mode start, revocable during Emergency mode, and must be revoked immediately upon its conclusion.  
- Delegated roles assigned during Emergency mode must be revoked immediately when Emergency mode ends; delegated roles assigned before Emergency mode remain active unless expired or revoked. Delegated roles assigned during Emergency mode may be revoked early before Emergency mode ends.  
- Administrators must update their credentials immediately after Emergency mode ends and before resuming any further system interaction; this credential update is an atomic, blocking process that must be completed by both administrators once per Emergency mode exit.  
- Emergency mode transitions, privilege grants/revocations, delegated role revocations, and credential update are modeled as atomic events to avoid interleaving ambiguities.
